    "description": "The Water Boss whole house descaler earns the top spot for a reason that has nothing to do with marketing: its listing is honest about what water it is designed for. Water Boss specifies hardness under 15 grains per gallon, alkalinity below 250 mg/L, pH under 8.3 and iron below 0.3 ppm, and it states plainly that the system does not reduce TDS, sulfur or iron. That is the single most useful set of numbers a salt-free conditioner can give you, because these units only work inside a window of water chemistry, and most competitors never say where the window is. Run a hardness and iron test first; if your results sit inside those limits, this is the one to buy. The listing rates it for up to 15 gallons per minute, which covers a busy household running a shower, a dishwasher and a washing machine at once, and it is described as suitable for families of four or more on city or well water. Media life is listed at 600,000 gallons or six years, backed by a six-year warranty. There is no salt, no backwash, no drain line and no electricity. The trade-off is the ceiling: above 15 grains of hardness, or with iron in the water, it is the wrong tool. Buy a salt-based softener instead if you want water that truly measures soft."
  },
  {
    "id": "watts-oneflow-plus",
    "name": "Watts Premier OneFlow Plus Salt-Free Scale and Filtration System (1-in)",
    "category": "TAC Cartridge, 250,000 Gal / 3 Years, Carbon Block Filter, 1-in NPT, Wall or Floor Mount",
    "badge": "Best Compact TAC",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Uses a TAC (template assisted crystallization) cartridge, per Watts",
      "Carbon block stage reduces chlorine taste and odor",
      "Compact 10 x 18 x 10 inch footprint with a mounting bracket",
      "1-inch NPT connections suit larger supply lines",
      "No salt or electricity required"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "TAC cartridge is rated for 250,000 gallons or 3 years, whichever comes first",
      "Two cartridges to track and replace",
      "Carbon block has a much shorter life than the TAC stage"
