    "bottomLine": "The best salt-free conditioner where floor space is tight and you also want chlorine taste reduced at the same time.",
    "description": "Most whole house conditioners are a tall tank that needs a corner of the basement. The Watts Premier OneFlow Plus is the exception: a two-cartridge system that the listing measures at 10 by 18 by 10 inches, with a bracket so it can be mounted on the floor or a wall right where the main line enters. That makes it the pick for utility closets, crawlspace entries and garages where a 50-inch tank will not fit. Watts names its media as TAC, template assisted crystallization, the salt-free method with the strongest independent track record, and rates the scale reduction cartridge for up to 250,000 gallons or three years. A second carbon block stage reduces chlorine taste and odor and catches sediment, so the system doubles as a basic whole house filter. Watts says to install it on the main supply before the water heater, which is exactly where scale control does the most good. The connections are 1-inch NPT, so it suits homes with larger supply lines. The trade-off is upkeep: cartridges are smaller than tank media, so you replace them more often than a tank system, and the carbon stage runs out well before the TAC stage. Choose a tank system like the Water Boss if you would rather forget about it for six years."
  },
  {
    "id": "oceanic-20-gpm-tac",
    "name": "Oceanic Water Systems Salt-Free Water Conditioner, 20 GPM TAC",
    "category": "TAC Tank, 20 GPM, For 4-8 Bathroom Homes, No Backwash, Made in USA",
    "badge": "Best for Large Homes",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Rated for 20 gallons per minute",
      "Designed for homes with 4 to 8 bathrooms, per the listing",
      "TAC media in a full-size tank",
      "No backwash and no wastewater",
      "Made in the USA, per the listing"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Large tank needs floor space and headroom",
      "Listing does not state hardness, iron or pH limits",
      "Oversized and unnecessary for small homes"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The conditioner to buy when a big house with several bathrooms needs real flow without a pressure drop.",
    "description": "The most common complaint about whole house water treatment in a large home is a pressure drop when several fixtures run at once. The Oceanic Water Systems 20 GPM unit is the answer to that problem. Its listing rates it for 20 gallons per minute and describes it as designed for homes with four to eight bathrooms, which is the highest flow rating among the picks here. Flow matters more than anything else on a conditioner, because TAC media needs enough contact time to work, and a tank sized too small for peak demand both drops pressure and pushes water through faster than the media is designed for. Oceanic lists TAC media, no backwash and no wastewater, so there is no drain to plumb and nothing to program. The listing also says it is made in the USA and states it can even remove existing scale in some cases, which is a claim to treat cautiously: owners commonly report gradual improvement rather than a clean pipe overnight. The real trade-off is what the listing does not say. It gives no hardness, iron or pH limits, so test your water and ask the seller before you commit, and expect TAC to struggle above roughly 15 grains per gallon. For one to three bathrooms, the Premier 12 GPM unit is the better-matched size."
  },
  {
    "id": "premier-12-gpm-tac",
    "name": "Premier Water Systems Scale Prevention Salt-Free Water Conditioner, 12 GPM TAC",
    "category": "TAC Tank, 12 GPM, For 1-3 Bathroom Homes, Bypass Valve Included, Made in USA",
    "badge": "Best for Average Homes",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Rated for 12 gallons per minute",
      "Sized for homes with 1 to 3 bathrooms",
      "Bypass valve included with the head",
      "No electricity, backwash, drain line or salt",
      "Simple pipe-in, pipe-out installation"
