        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best water conditioner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes with moderate hardness, the Water Boss WB-WH-DSCLR is the best water conditioner, because it is rated for 15 gallons per minute and 600,000 gallons and its listing states the hardness, iron, pH and alkalinity it is designed for. Choose the Watts OneFlow Plus for tight spaces and the Oceanic 20 GPM for large homes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a water conditioner remove hardness?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A salt-free water conditioner leaves calcium and magnesium in the water and changes them into microscopic crystals that are less likely to form scale. Hardness tests read essentially the same afterward. If you want hardness actually removed, you need an ion exchange water softener, which swaps those minerals for sodium or potassium and needs salt and a drain.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a water conditioner the same as a water softener?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A softener removes hardness minerals through ion exchange and needs salt, a drain and power. A conditioner, usually using template assisted crystallization, keeps minerals in the water but reduces scale, with no salt, drain or electricity. Softeners give truly soft water; conditioners give less scale on pipes, fixtures and heating equipment.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do salt-free water conditioners really work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                TAC and similar crystallization media have the strongest independent evidence among salt-free options and reduce scale substantially when water sits within the designed range, typically under about 15 grains per gallon with little iron. Magnetic and electronic clamp-on devices have much weaker evidence. Results also depend on correct sizing and protecting the media from iron and heavy chlorine.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a salt-free water conditioner last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The tank and head can last many years, but the media is replaced on a schedule. The listings here rate media at 250,000 gallons or three years for the Watts cartridge, 600,000 gallons or six years for the Water Boss and 900,000 gallons for the Tier1. Hard water and heavy use shorten those intervals.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a water conditioner on well water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if the water is within the media limits. Water Boss lists its system for city and well water, but published limits call for iron under 0.3 ppm, and many wells exceed that. Test for iron, manganese, hardness and pH first, and install an iron filter ahead of the conditioner if needed, or choose a softener.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where should a water conditioner be installed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On the main water line after the shutoff and before the water heater, so every hot water appliance receives conditioned water. Fit a bypass valve for servicing. Put any sediment or carbon filter ahead of the conditioner, and if possible leave outdoor hose bibs upstream so irrigation water does not use up media capacity.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
